@node Overview
@chapter Overview
@cindex overview

raspdash is a single program, written in C++23, that draws on a terminal
with ncurses.  On the Pi it runs inside a full-screen alacritty window
that the X session starts after automatic login (@pxref{Installing on the
Pi}).

The dashboard has eight kinds of @dfn{screens}, shown one at a time (the
markets take three tabs, so there are ten):

@enumerate
@item
@strong{Machines}: load, CPU, memory, disks and temperatures of the
computers on the local network and of the router.
@item
@strong{Uptime}: how long each of those machines has been running, its
recent boots over the last week, and the power cuts deduced from
machines restarting together.
@item
@strong{Network}: the devices on the local network and the services
(SSH, HTTP, @dots{}) each one offers, found by a port scan.
@item
@strong{Tasks}: read-only Google Tasks lists, chosen in the configuration.
@item
@strong{Timetable}: this week's classes of a group at Peter the Great
St.@: Petersburg Polytechnic University, from @uref{https://ruz.spbstu.ru}.
@item
@strong{Weather}: forecasts from MET Norway for configured places.
@item
@strong{Markets}: candlestick charts with moving averages over a month
and half a year, on two
screens (XAUT and TON; BTC and USD/RUB), and a chart comparing them.
@item
@strong{XMR}: Monero on one page: market data, a 30-day price chart,
the network, a mining calculator and the
state of your own @command{monerod}.
@end enumerate

@menu
* Sources and caching::         How and when data is fetched.
@end menu

@node Sources and caching
@section Sources and caching
@cindex sources
@cindex caching
@cindex lazy fetching
@cindex refresh

Each screen gets its data from one or more @dfn{sources}.  Every source
runs on its own thread, so the screens only draw what the sources
already have, and a slow or broken network never freezes the display.

Fetching is @dfn{lazy}.  Each source has an interval, and fetches again
only when its data is older than that:

@multitable @columnfractions .3 .3 .4
@headitem Screen @tab Default interval @tab Also fetched when
@item Machines @tab 10 seconds @tab ---
@item Uptime @tab 1 minute @tab ---
@item Network @tab 10 minutes @tab ---
@item Tasks @tab 1 hour @tab ---
@item Timetable @tab 3 days @tab a new week starts
@item Weather @tab 2 hours @tab ---
@item Markets @tab 12 hours @tab ---
@item XMR market @tab 10 minutes @tab ---
@item XMR chart @tab 30 minutes @tab ---
@item XMR network @tab 2 minutes @tab ---
@item XMR node @tab 30 seconds @tab ---
@end multitable

What a source fetches is also saved in the cache directory
(@file{~/.cache/raspdash} by default; @pxref{Application settings}).
When raspdash starts, every source first loads its cached data, so the
screens are filled at once, even without a network, and the interval
counts from when that data was fetched, not from the start.  Restarting
the dashboard or rebooting the Pi therefore does not cause a burst of
requests.

When a fetch fails, the screen keeps showing the last good data and the
footer says how old it is and what went wrong.  The source tries again
after one minute, then after two, four, and so on, up to its interval or
30 minutes, whichever is shorter.  Press @kbd{r} to fetch the current
screen's data at once.

@cindex rotation
The screens take turns in the same terminal window: each is shown for
60 seconds, then the terminal is cleared and the next one is drawn.  Pressing any key pauses the rotation for
a while (@pxref{Application settings}).

@node Building
@chapter Building
@cindex building
@cindex requirements

raspdash uses the GNU build system.  It needs:

@itemize
@item
A C++23 compiler whose library has @code{<print>}, @code{std::expected}
and @code{std::ranges::to}: GCC 14 or later.  @command{configure} checks
for this and stops if the compiler is too old.
@item
ncurses with wide-character support (@code{ncursesw}), libcurl,
OpenSSL's @code{libcrypto} and the nlohmann-json headers.  On Debian
and derivatives these are the packages @code{libncurses-dev},
@code{libcurl4-openssl-dev}, @code{libssl-dev} and
@code{nlohmann-json3-dev}.
@item
@command{pkg-config} and, when building from a git checkout, Autoconf,
Automake, @code{autoconf-archive} and Texinfo.
@end itemize

The TOML parser, toml++, is included in @file{third_party/}.

From a git checkout, generate the build system first:

@example
./bootstrap
@end example

@noindent
Then build as usual; building in a separate directory works too:

@example
./configure
make
make check
make install
@end example

@cindex @option{--enable-static-libstdcxx}
@command{configure} accepts the standard options and one of its own:

@table @option
@item --enable-static-libstdcxx
Link the C++ runtime (libstdc++ and libgcc) statically.  Use this when
the compiler is newer than the system's own, as on Raspberry Pi OS
Bullseye with a self-built GCC: otherwise the program would look for
the newer @file{libstdc++.so} at run time and fail to start.  A
@option{-latomic} requested by a library (static OpenSSL does) is
turned into the compiler's @file{libatomic.a} for the same reason.
@item --enable-static-deps
@cindex @option{--enable-static-deps}
Ask @command{pkg-config} for static link flags (@samp{pkg-config
--static}), for linking against static builds of ncursesw, libcurl and
OpenSSL such as the private ones described in
@file{deploy/TASK-build-deps.md}.  With a distribution's libcurl, which is
built with many optional features, the static flags name libraries that
may not be installed; leave the option off there.
@end table

To use a compiler that is not the default, name it:

@example
./configure CXX=/opt/gcc-16.2.0/bin/g++-16 --enable-static-libstdcxx
@end example

@command{make install} installs @command{raspdash}, @command{raspdash-auth}
(@pxref{Tasks}), this manual, the statistics script used by the Machines
screen and a sample configuration in @file{@var{datadir}/raspdash}.

@node Installing on the Pi
@chapter Installing on the Pi
@cindex Raspberry Pi
@cindex kiosk
@cindex autostart

The Pi runs Raspberry Pi OS with the LXDE desktop and automatic login.
raspdash starts with the desktop session: an XDG autostart entry runs
@command{raspdash-kiosk}, which opens a full-screen, borderless alacritty
window running @command{raspdash}, and starts it again a few seconds
after it exits or crashes.

The simplest way to set this up is the script in the source tree.  Run
it on the Pi from a checkout, naming the C++23 compiler if it is not the
default:

@example
CXX=/opt/gcc-16.2.0/bin/g++-16 deploy/install-pi.sh
@end example

@noindent
It builds in @file{_build/}, runs the test suite, installs into
@file{~/.local} (no root needed), and then:

@itemize
@item
installs @file{deploy/raspdash-kiosk} as @file{~/.local/bin/raspdash-kiosk}
and @file{deploy/alacritty-kiosk.toml} into
@file{~/.local/share/raspdash/};
@item
writes @file{~/.config/autostart/raspdash.desktop};
@item
links @file{~/.config/raspdash/config.toml} to the checkout's
@file{config.toml}, so editing the file in the checkout is enough.
@end itemize

Set @env{PREFIX} to install elsewhere and @env{BUILD_DIR} to build
elsewhere.

@menu
* Kiosk launcher::              @command{raspdash-kiosk}.
* Fonts::                       The terminal font and icons.
* Updating::                    Installing a new version.
@end menu

@node Kiosk launcher
@section The kiosk launcher
@cindex @command{raspdash-kiosk}
@cindex screensaver

@command{raspdash-kiosk} turns off screen blanking (@samp{xset s off
-dpms}) and stops xscreensaver, which the default LXDE session starts,
so the dashboard stays visible.  Set @env{RASPDASH_KEEP_SCREENSAVER} to
@samp{1} to keep them.  It hides the mouse pointer when
@command{unclutter} is installed.

The autostart entry starts the launcher together with the window
manager.  A window that opens before the window manager runs loses its
full-screen request and stays at alacritty's default 800 by 600 pixels,
so the launcher first waits, for up to 30 seconds, until
@command{xprop} sees an EWMH window manager on the root window
(@samp{_NET_SUPPORTING_WM_CHECK}).  Without @command{xprop} it does not
wait.

@cindex restart
The launcher restarts raspdash every 3 hours, so an instance that hangs
or slowly goes wrong does not stay on the screen.  It runs raspdash
under @command{timeout}, which sends @code{SIGTERM}; raspdash quits
cleanly on it and comes back three seconds later, with the data its
sources cached.  An instance that does not quit within two minutes is
killed.  Set @env{RASPDASH_RESTART} to change the period, in the form
@command{timeout} takes (@samp{90m}, @samp{6h}), or to @samp{0} to
turn the restarts off.

Other variables it reads:

@table @env
@item RASPDASH
The raspdash binary to run (default: @command{raspdash} from
@env{PATH}).
@item RASPDASH_CONFIG
The configuration file, passed on to raspdash.
@item RASPDASH_TERM_CONFIG
The alacritty configuration.  By default it uses
@file{alacritty-kiosk.toml} next to the script or in
@file{../share/raspdash/}.
@item RASPDASH_RESTART
How long each raspdash runs before it is restarted (default
@samp{3h}; @samp{0} never restarts).
@end table

It logs starts and exits to
@file{~/.local/state/raspdash/kiosk.log}.

The alacritty configuration is kept apart from the user's own
configuration.  It is in TOML, the format of alacritty 0.13 and later;
alacritty 0.17 still reads YAML but shows a deprecation banner across
the window, and ignores the full-screen setting under the Pi's
@command{mutter}.

@node Fonts
@section Fonts
@cindex fonts
@cindex Nerd Font
@cindex icons

The kiosk terminal uses @samp{DejaVuSansM Nerd Font Mono}: DejaVu Sans
Mono, which has the Cyrillic, box-drawing and Braille characters
raspdash draws, patched with the Nerd Fonts icon sets (weather symbols,
hardware, logos).  The @samp{Mono} variant keeps every icon one cell
wide, which is what the C library's @code{wcwidth} reports for them, so
layouts line up.  At size 11 a 1920x1080 screen holds about
190 columns by 54 lines.

Install it for the user running the kiosk:

@example
mkdir -p ~/.local/share/fonts/DejaVuSansMNerd
curl -L https://github.com/ryanoasis/nerd-fonts/releases/latest/download/DejaVuSansMono.tar.xz \
  | tar -xJ -C ~/.local/share/fonts/DejaVuSansMNerd
fc-cache -f ~/.local/share/fonts
@end example

On a terminal without a Nerd Font, set @code{icons = "unicode"} in
@code{[app]} (@pxref{Application settings}) and raspdash uses standard
Unicode symbols instead.

@node Updating
@section Updating
@cindex updating

Pull or copy the new sources onto the Pi and run
@file{deploy/install-pi.sh} again.  Then restart the dashboard: press
@kbd{q} (when @code{allow_quit} is on) and the launcher starts the new
binary, or log out and back in.

@node Invoking raspdash
@chapter Invoking raspdash
@cindex invoking
@cindex options

@example
raspdash [@var{option}]@dots{}
@end example

@table @option
@item -c @var{file}
@itemx --config=@var{file}
Read the configuration from @var{file}.
@item -s @var{name}
@itemx --screen=@var{name}
Start on the screen whose tab title is @var{name}, such as
@samp{Weather}.
@item --check
Fetch every source once, ignoring the cache's age, print one line per
source (@samp{ok} or @samp{FAIL} with the reason) and exit with status 1
if any failed.  This needs no terminal, so it is handy over
@command{ssh} after changing the configuration.
@item -h
@itemx --help
Print a usage summary and exit.
@item -V
@itemx --version
Print the version and exit.
@end table

@cindex configuration file, location
Without @option{--config}, raspdash uses the first of these that exists:
@env{RASPDASH_CONFIG}; @file{config.toml} in the current directory;
@file{$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/raspdash/config.toml} (by default
@file{~/.config/raspdash/config.toml}); and
@file{@var{sysconfdir}/raspdash/config.toml}.

@menu
* Keys::                        Keyboard commands.
@end menu

@node Keys
@section Keys
@cindex keys

@table @kbd
@item 1 @dots{} 9
@itemx 0
Show that screen; @kbd{0} shows the tenth.
@item @key{RIGHT}
@itemx @key{TAB}
@itemx @key{SPC}
@itemx l
@itemx Left mouse button
Next screen.  A click anywhere on the screen works, so a mouse alone
is enough to page through the screens.  raspdash asks the terminal for
SGR mouse reports (modes 1000 and 1006) and reads them itself rather
than through ncurses: ncurses 6.2, the Pi's version, waits without a
time limit for the rest of a mouse report it has started to read, and
a report that never completes froze the whole dashboard.  raspdash
gives a report 50 milliseconds and drops it if it is incomplete.
@item @key{LEFT}
@itemx S-@key{TAB}
@itemx h
Previous screen.
@item r
Fetch the current screen's data now.
@item q
Quit, when @code{allow_quit} is on.  Under the kiosk launcher raspdash
starts again a few seconds later.
@end table

The footer shows, for each source of the current screen, how long ago
it last succeeded, or the latest error.  On the Machines screen, hosts
that do not answer are left out of the footer as well.

@node Configuration
@chapter Configuration
@cindex configuration
@cindex @file{config.toml}

The configuration is one TOML file, @file{config.toml} in the source
tree.  It is personal (host addresses, task lists, places), so git
ignores it; the tracked @file{config.example.toml} is the starting
point:

@example
cp config.example.toml config.toml
@end example

@noindent
@file{deploy/install-pi.sh} makes this copy itself when
@file{config.toml} does not exist yet.

Each screen reads its own table; the @code{[app]} table holds
settings for the whole program.  Relative paths in the file are
relative to the directory holding the file (after following symbolic
links), so @samp{secrets/token.json} means the @file{secrets} directory
beside @file{config.toml}.  A leading @samp{~/} means the home directory.

Every screen's table takes an @code{interval} key: the number of seconds
between fetches (@pxref{Sources and caching}).  The XMR screen has one
such key per source instead, such as @code{market_interval}
(@pxref{XMR}).

Changes take effect when raspdash starts again.

@menu
* Application settings::        The @code{[app]} table.
@end menu

@node Application settings
@section Application settings
@cindex @code{[app]}

@table @code
@item screens
The screens to show, in tab order: any of @samp{"machines"},
@samp{"tasks"}, @samp{"timetable"}, @samp{"weather"} and
@samp{"markets"}.  Leave one out to hide it.  Default: all five.
@item rotate_seconds
Show each screen for this many seconds, then clear the terminal and
show the next one; @samp{0} turns rotation off.  Default: 60.
@item pause_after_key_seconds
After a key press, do not rotate for this many seconds.  Default: 120.
@item allow_quit
Whether @kbd{q} quits.  Default: true.
@item icons
@samp{"nerd"} to draw Nerd Font icons, @samp{"unicode"} for standard
Unicode symbols only (@pxref{Fonts}).  Default: @samp{"nerd"}.
@item cache_dir
Where fetched data is kept between runs.  Default:
@file{$XDG_CACHE_HOME/raspdash}, that is @file{~/.cache/raspdash}.
Deleting the directory makes every source fetch again at the next start.
@item clock_format
The header clock, as a @code{std::format} chrono specification such as
@samp{"%a %d %b  %H:%M"}.  The clock uses the system time zone.
@item show_ip
Whether the header shows this machine's IPv4 address on the local
network, left of the clock.  It is the address of the interface that
carries the default route, read again on every redraw, so it follows a
DHCP change or a switch from Ethernet to Wi-Fi; @samp{no network} in red
means no interface has an address.  Default @code{true}.
@item user_agent
The @samp{User-Agent} header sent with HTTP requests.  MET Norway's
terms ask for one that identifies the application.  Default:
@samp{raspdash/@var{version} (+https://github.com/mb6ockatf/raspdash)}.
@end table

HTTP requests honour the usual @env{http_proxy}, @env{https_proxy} and
@env{no_proxy} environment variables.

@node Secrets
@chapter Secrets
@cindex secrets
@cindex credentials

Credentials and tokens go into the @file{secrets/} directory beside
@file{config.toml}.  Git ignores everything in it except its
@file{README.md}, so they are never committed.  Keep the files readable
only by you (@samp{chmod 600}).

@table @file
@item secrets/google_client_secret.json
The OAuth client downloaded from Google Cloud (@pxref{Tasks}).
@item secrets/google_token.json
The refresh token written by @command{raspdash-auth}.
@item secrets/keenetic.toml
The router's login (@pxref{Machines}).
@end table

