@node Adding a screen
@section Adding a screen
@cindex adding a screen

A screen module defines a @code{Source} subclass and a @code{Screen}
subclass, plus an install function that reads the module's
configuration table:

@itemize
@item
The source's @code{fetch} runs on its own thread.  It does blocking I/O,
stores the result with @code{Latest<T>::set}, saves the raw response with
@code{cache_put}, and throws on failure.  Keep the parsing in a separate
function that takes the response body, so that tests can feed it saved
responses.
@item
@code{restore} rebuilds the result from @code{cache_get} when raspdash
starts and returns when that data was fetched.  @code{expired} may force
a fetch before the interval is up.
@item
The screen's @code{draw} runs on the UI thread several times a second.
It takes the latest result with @code{Latest<T>::get} and must never
block.  Draw icons with @code{tui::icon}, giving a Unicode fallback.
@item
Declare the install function in @file{src/modules.hpp}, add it to the
@code{modules} table there, and list the new files in
@file{Makefile.am}.
@end itemize

@file{src/timetable.cpp} is a small, complete example.

@node Internals
@section Internals
@cindex internals

The source files carry no comments apart from their license notice;
this section is where their contracts are written down.

@subsection Sources
@cindex @code{Source}
@cindex @code{Latest}

@code{Latest<T>} holds the newest value of a result.  @code{set} replaces
it; @code{get} returns a @code{std::shared_ptr<const T>} snapshot that
stays valid however long the reader keeps it, even if a newer value
arrives meanwhile.  It is the only data shared between a source's thread
and the UI thread.

@code{Source} is constructed with a name (shown in the footer and used
for cache file names), an interval and a retry delay (one minute by
default, never more than the interval).

@table @code
@item start ()
@itemx stop ()
Start and stop the poller thread.  @code{stop} wakes the thread, asks it
to finish and joins it.  The owner must call @code{stop} before the
object is destroyed, because the thread calls the subclass's virtual
functions; every subclass destructor calls @code{stop} too.
@item status ()
A copy of @code{Status}: @code{updated} (last success), @code{error_at}
(last failure), @code{error} (empty after a success) and @code{busy}.
@code{has_data} is true once anything was fetched or restored.
@item refresh_now ()
Wake the poller to fetch at once.
@item poll_once ()
Fetch synchronously on the calling thread and record the outcome; used
by @option{--check}, never together with @code{start}.
@item fetch ()
Implemented by the subclass; runs on the poller thread, may block,
stores the result and throws on failure.
@item restore ()
Called once on the poller thread before anything else.  The subclass
rebuilds its result from the cache and returns the time that data was
fetched, or @code{std::nullopt}.  An exception counts as
@code{std::nullopt}.
@item expired (@var{now})
Return true to fetch before the interval is up.  It is asked at least
every five minutes; an exception counts as true.
@item cache_put (@var{key}, @var{body})
@itemx cache_get (@var{key})
Store and load one blob in @file{@var{cache_dir}/@var{name}-@var{key}}
(characters other than letters, digits, @samp{-}, @samp{_} and @samp{.}
become @samp{_}).  Writing goes through a temporary file and a rename,
so a crash never leaves half a file.  The file's modification time is
the @code{saved} time @code{cache_get} returns.  Failures are ignored.
@code{Source::set_cache_dir} sets the directory once, from @code{App}.
@end table

The poller loop: a fetch is due when @code{refresh_now} was called, when
there is no data, when the data is older than the interval, or when
@code{expired} says so.  While the last fetch failed, the next attempt
waits for the backoff instead, which starts at the retry delay and
doubles after every further failure up to the interval or 30 minutes.
Between checks the thread sleeps on a condition variable until the next
due time, at most five minutes, and wakes early for @code{refresh_now}
or @code{stop}.

@subsection Screens and the application
@cindex @code{Screen}
@cindex @code{App}

A @code{Screen} has a @code{title} for its tab, a list of
@code{sources} for the @kbd{r} key and, unless it overrides
@code{footer_sources}, for the footer, @code{draw}, which
paints inside the rectangle between the header and footer lines, and
@code{key}, which receives keys the application does not use and
returns whether it used them.  @code{draw} is called about four times a
second and must not block; an exception from it is shown in place of
the screen.

@code{App} owns the sources and screens.  @code{add_source} returns a
typed reference so a screen can keep it.  @code{shared<T> (@var{key},
@var{make})} returns an object that several modules share, creating it
with @var{make} on first use; it lives as long as the application.  The
Machines and Uptime screens use it for the host sources.  @code{run}
starts every
source, then loops: draw the header (tabs and clock), the current
screen and the footer; wait up to 250 ms for a key; rotate when due.
SIGINT, SIGTERM and SIGHUP end the loop cleanly.  @code{check} runs
every source's @code{poll_once} in parallel and prints the results.

@subsection Drawing
@cindex @code{tui}

@code{tui::init} sets the locale from the environment and, if that
locale is not UTF-8, switches character handling to @samp{C.UTF-8}; it
sets @env{ESCDELAY} to 25 ms and starts ncurses with default colors.
Colors are referred to by role (@code{NORMAL}, @code{DIM},
@code{TITLE}, @code{ACCENT}, @code{GOOD}, @code{WARN}, @code{BAD},
@code{FRAME}, the tab roles and four chart series) through
@code{tui::attr}; with 256 colors the roles get a richer palette.
@code{tui::level} maps a fraction to @code{GOOD}, @code{WARN} (from 0.7)
or @code{BAD} (from 0.9).

Text is UTF-8 throughout.  @code{width} counts terminal columns,
@code{fit} truncates to a width ending in @samp{…}, @code{pad} pads or
truncates to an exact width, and @code{put} draws clipped to a width and
to the screen.  Invalid UTF-8 shows as U+FFFD.

@code{Rect} is a rectangle of cells with helpers to shrink it
(@code{inset}), cut rows off it (@code{top}, @code{below}) and split it
into columns or rows with gaps, the last part taking the leftover
cells.  @code{frame} draws a rounded box with a title and returns the
inside.  @code{gauge} draws a bar with eighth-cell resolution over a
dotted track.

@code{Braille} is a canvas of 2@tie{}by@tie{}4 dots per cell, addressed
from the top left; @code{set} and @code{line} (Bresenham) set dots and
@code{draw} paints only the cells that have some.  @code{tui::icon}
returns its first argument when Nerd Font icons are on and the second
otherwise.

@subsection HTTP, times and configuration

@code{http::perform} sends a @code{Request} and returns the
@code{Response}; it throws @code{http::Error} only for transport
failures, not for HTTP error statuses.  Header names in the response are
lower case.  @code{http::get} also throws for a non-2xx status, quoting
the start of the body.  Redirects are followed, responses are
decompressed, and proxies come from the environment.
@code{http::global_init} sets the User-Agent for all requests.

@code{util::zone} looks up an IANA time zone (the system's for an empty
name) and throws if it is unknown; @code{util::to_local} converts a time
point to that zone's wall clock.  @code{util::parse_iso8601} accepts
@samp{YYYY-MM-DD}, optionally followed by @samp{T} or a space and
@samp{HH:MM[:SS[.fraction]]}, and an offset @samp{Z}, @samp{+HH:MM} or
@samp{-HH:MM}; without an offset the time is UTC.
@code{util::format_age} gives ages such as @samp{5s}, @samp{12m},
@samp{3h} and @samp{2d}.

@code{Config::section} returns an empty table for a missing section, so
modules can read keys with defaults unconditionally.  toml++ is compiled
once, in @file{src/config.cpp}, with @code{TOML_IMPLEMENTATION}; every
other file sees it with @code{TOML_HEADER_ONLY=0}.

@subsection Test harness

@file{tests/check.hpp} provides @code{CHECK (@var{condition})}, which
reports the file and line of a failing check, @code{check::data}, which
reads a fixture from @file{tests/data} through @env{srcdir} (so VPATH
builds work), and @code{check::run}, which turns an escaped exception
into exit status 99 (a hard error for Automake).  A test exits with 77
to be reported as skipped.

@node Coding style
@section Coding style
@cindex coding style
@cindex astyle
@cindex pre-commit hook

The code follows the GNU style as Artistic Style formats it with its
default GNU settings (@samp{astyle --style=gnu}).  A git pre-commit hook
in @file{hooks/pre-commit} formats staged C++ files and stages the
result; enable it once per clone with

@example
git config core.hooksPath hooks
@end example

@noindent
If a file has both staged and unstaged changes the hook does not touch
it; it refuses the commit and tells you to format and stage the file.
Code in @file{third_party/} is left alone.

The code must build with GCC 14, which is what the development laptop
has, as well as with the newer GCC on the Pi.

@node Tests
@section Tests
@cindex tests

@samp{make check} runs one test program per module from
@file{tests/}.  The tests parse saved responses from
@file{tests/data/} and do not use the network.  For a live check of
every source, run @samp{raspdash --check}.

